    "summary": "Scorching Brent era set with fiery The Other One and Terrapin Station",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a high‑energy second set highlighted by an incendiary The Other One, Terrapin Station and a soaring Foolish Heart. Jerry’s guitar is on fire, Brent’s keyboards drive the band forward, and Phil’s bass shines throughout.",
    "review": "On May 6 1990 at Cal State Dominguez Hills the Dead were in full‑blown Brent‑era mode. The first set closed strong with a heartfelt Foolish Heart and an exuberant Feel Like A Stranger, but it was the second set that cemented this night’s reputation – Terrapin Station surged into a cosmic Space, then erupted into a blistering The Other One that rode on Brent’s psychedelic keys and Jerry’s searing leads. Standing On The Moon followed with a crisp, soaring solo, while Throwing Stones kept the momentum rolling to an uplifting Turn On Your Lovelight encore. Across all available tapes the band sounds tight despite the desert heat; Phil’s bass lines were described as “walking across the room,” Brent’s keyboards were “on fire” and driving the groove, and Bob’s vocal quirks added extra flavor on The Other One. Even where the flat soundboard mix shows its age, the performance shines through – a must‑listen for fans of this hot summer run.\n\nThe audience FOH capture (gd90-05-06.schoeps-fob…) offers the clearest window into that energy, but the full‑set SBD from Wiley remains the most complete source. Either way, this concert stands out as one of the high points of the 1990 tour, showcasing a band fully in sync and delivering several classic moments that still resonate today.",
